---
/**
 * website/src/components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro — the flow (website plan, Task W1.4
 * step 3; redesign part P8, findings RF12, RF20 and RF21; decision D185, superseded in part
 * by the changes spec of 2026-10-18, item 4).
 *
 * **The four steps the changes spec names.** The owner's second brief draws the run as
 * `1 Your agent` -> `2 Fugaro` -> `3 Ephemeral cloud job` -> `4 Pull request`, one short
 * line each, and this component is that picture. It supersedes D185's step names (which
 * drew the flow as `task submitted -> ephemeral Cloud Run job -> agent runs -> pull
 * request`): the spec's item 4 is the later ruling, and its four steps say what the site is
 * for — a reader's agent is where a task starts, and Fugaro is what carries it to the
 * cloud, rather than a `fugaro run` invocation the reader is asked to type. `fugaro run`
 * stays in the documentation as the manual option; `website/test/home.mjs` reads the four
 * names out of the spec's own item 4 rather than out of a copy of them here.
 *
 * **Every caption is a line the repository already writes.** A caption goes through
 * `comparable()` below at build time and the build stops, naming the file, when it is not in
 * the section it cites; `website/test/home.mjs` makes the same check against the built page
 * from outside. Nothing here is written for the page.
 *
 * **Why a caption cites a section rather than the whole file.** Three of the four captions
 * are lines of README.md, and held to the file alone they could be shuffled among the steps
 * with each still a quotation — while the page then said Fugaro is where the pull request
 * opens. Each cites the section it belongs to (the agent's line is from `## Getting
 * started`, Fugaro's from `## Why Fugaro`, the job's from `## How it works`), and inside one
 * section the lines must appear in the order the section writes them, which is the rule
 * `website/test/home.mjs` holds this page to.
 *
 * **The pull request's caption is the one that cites another document** (the owner's
 * feedback of 2026-10-19). It used to be README.md's step 4 clause about the branch — "each
 * commit is pushed within about a minute while the agent works" — which overstates what a
 * reader of the picture takes it to mean: what opens the pull request is the run's first
 * checkpoint push, and what a reader gets then is a draft, marked not verified. README.md's
 * step 4 says that second half too ("a draft opens at the first push (marked not verified)"),
 * but the sentence that says when the pull request opens — the run's first checkpoint push,
 * within about a minute of the run's first commit — is `docs/git-providers.md`'s, which is
 * the document that owns the behaviour, so the step quotes it there. What the caption cannot
 * carry is the rest of the fact: the minute before that push, which is the section above it in
 * that document, and the checkpoints the run keeps pushing as it works, which is another
 * sentence of the section it cites. A caption is one contiguous quotation of a repository
 * document, and one stitched from two sentences' halves is a sentence the repository never
 * wrote — so the step, which is the pull request, says what opens and in what state, and the
 * rest is the section it cites.
 *
 * **The flow is a list, and its picture is decoration.** The steps are an `<ol>` of
 * headings and captions, which is the whole content, and each step's arrow is an SVG marked
 * `aria-hidden`, because a picture of four boxes and three arrows is not a thing a screen
 * reader should have to describe when the four boxes are right there as text. Nothing about
 * the flow depends on the arrows having been drawn.
 *
 * **One picture, three widths.** A phone reads the run top to bottom, one card under the
 * other, with an arrow between each pair (below 35 rem); a tablet or a laptop reads it as a
 * two-by-two — `1 2` over `4 3`, the run going right, then down, then right again along the
 * bottom row — which is the
 * shape a 768 px screen wants, where four stacked cards are over 400 px of picture and four
 * across is not yet possible (35 rem to 72 rem); and at the page's own maximum width the
 * four cards stand in the one row the run travels along, left to right (72 rem up).
 *
 * **The two-by-two's bottom row is `4 3` and not `3 4`.** Reading the grid row-major would
 * put step 3 in the cell under step 2, where the down arrow is drawn — and that arrow leaves
 * step 2, so the cell it lands in has to be step 3's: with `3 4` it landed on step 4, and the
 * picture read `1 -> 2 -> 4` with step 3 pointing at nothing. The two bottom steps are placed
 * by hand for that reason, and step 3's arrow turns back along the row to step 4. The DOM
 * order is untouched — `1 2 3 4` is the run's order and what a screen reader reads; only the
 * drawing snakes. `website/test/base-styles.mjs` reads the cells the four steps occupy and
 * holds each arrow to the side its successor's cell is on, so the two cannot disagree again.
 *
 * **Why 72 rem, and not the chrome's 48 rem or the 64 rem this used to be.** Four cards
 * share the row, so a card is a quarter of the column less the gaps between them, and the
 * **longest step name has to fit one line** — `Ephemeral cloud job` is the spec's own third
 * step and it may not be printed as `Ephemeral cloud / job` on a desktop while the three
 * names beside it are one line each: a card that wraps is a card taller than its neighbours,
 * which is the ragged row the review of this branch measured. The arithmetic that says
 * where that is true is `website/test/base-styles.mjs`'s: it computes the card's content
 * box from the built stylesheet — the page's column, the four cards, the three gaps and the
 * card's own padding and border, every number read rather than typed — and refuses a row
 * where the longest name (or the longest word of any name, which is what a wrapped name
 * cannot break) does not fit. At 72 rem, which is also where the page stops growing, that
 * box is 232 px against the longest name's estimated 225. So the row is drawn exactly where
 * the page has its full column and nowhere below it.
 *
 * **The arrow is absolutely positioned, so it costs the card nothing.** The arrow used to
 * sit in the row's flow, 32 px wide inside each step, which took that 32 px out of three of
 * the four cards — the fourth step's card was a quarter of the row wider than the other
 * three, and the least the third card could be was 32 px narrower than the name it had to
 * print. It now sits *in the gap* between two cards, `left: 100%` from the step before it,
 * and the gap is what it is drawn in. It is still the same SVG, still turned by `transform`
 * alone (the transformation the arrow-direction check reads), and the step is
 * `position: relative` so the arrow is placed against it.
 *
 * **The cards are one height, and their content starts at the top.** A row of cards whose
 * bottoms do not line up reads as a broken row, so the steps stretch to the row's height,
 * the cards stretch to their step's, and the card's own content — the number, the name and
 * the caption — is packed from the card's top rather than spread down it.
 *
 * **The name may wrap, and the number does not take its room.** A step name is a heading and
 * not a label, so it wraps rather than running out of its card — `white-space: nowrap` here
 * was the bug the arithmetic above was written for. The number sits beside the name only in
 * the phone's one-column stack, where a card has the page's whole width; from 35 rem up —
 * the two-by-two and the row alike — it sits *above* the name, because it is `flex: none`
 * and 32 px plus the head's gap is 44 px of a quarter-width card's content box, which is the
 * difference between `Ephemeral cloud job` on one line and on two.
 *
 * **The animation.** The steps fade in as the reader scrolls to them, using a scroll-driven
 * animation (`animation-timeline: view()`), which costs no JavaScript at all. It is inside a
 * feature query and a preference query: a browser that cannot run scroll-driven animations,
 * and a reader whose system asks for less motion, both get the flow as it stands — every
 * step visible, nothing moving (W1.4 step 3, changes spec item 4's reduced-motion rule).
 */
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import { REPO_ROOT } from '../content.ts'

/**
 * A string with its markdown markers and its whitespace removed: how a caption and the
 * section it claims to come from are compared. Emphasis and code spans are markers rather
 * than text, and where a document breaks a line or pads a column is its layout rather than
 * its words — most of the README's ASCII diagram is padding, so a caption quoted from it is
 * compared with the drawing taken out.
 */
function comparable(document: string): string {
  return document.replace(/[`*]/g, '').replace(/\s+/g, '')
}

/**
 * One `## <heading>` section of a document of this repository, up to the next `## ` — the
 * same reader `website/test/home.mjs` uses, so the build and the check resolve a section the
 * same way. A document or a heading the repository does not carry is a defect in this file
 * rather than in the document: the steps cite sections by name, and a renamed heading has to
 * fail here, naming itself.
 */
function section(document: string, heading: string): string {
  const text = readFileSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, document), 'utf8')
  // A heading's code span is a marker rather than part of its name, so the search reads the
  // headings with their backticks off: the section a step cites as `#Checkpoint pushes
  // (git.pr.checkpoints)` is the document's `## Checkpoint pushes (\`git.pr.checkpoints\`)`,
  // and the key in it is part of the name rather than something to drop.
  const at = [...text.matchAll(/^## (.+)$/gm)].find((match) => match[1].replace(/`/g, '') === heading)
  if (!at || at.index === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro: ${document} carries no "## ${heading}" section, and a step's caption is quoted from it (changes spec 2026-10-18, item 4; the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19). Either the section was renamed or the caption moved.`,
    )
  }
  const rest = text.slice(at.index)
  const next = rest.indexOf('\n## ', 1)
  return next === -1 ? rest : rest.slice(0, next)
}

/**
 * The sections a caption may come from, by the name each step cites. Sections rather than
 * whole files: three of the four captions are lines of README.md, so held to the file alone
 * they could be shuffled among the steps with every one of them still a quotation.
 */
const SOURCES: { [name: string]: string } = {
  'README.md#Getting started': section('README.md', 'Getting started'),
  'README.md#Why Fugaro': section('README.md', 'Why Fugaro'),
  'README.md#How it works': section('README.md', 'How it works'),
  'docs/git-providers.md#Checkpoint pushes (git.pr.checkpoints)': section('docs/git-providers.md', 'Checkpoint pushes (git.pr.checkpoints)'),
}

/**
 * The spec's four steps, in its order, each with the section its caption is a line of. The
 * names are the spec's own, spelled as it spells them; the captions are the repository's own
 * sentences, shortened to one line where a step is longer than a line wants to be.
 */
const STEPS = [
  {
    name: 'Your agent',
    source: 'README.md#Getting started',
    // README.md's own sentence about who starts a run: the plugin's skill, in the reader's
    // agent, is where a task goes in.
    caption: "the plugin's working skill launches, watches and diagnoses runs",
  },
  {
    name: 'Fugaro',
    source: 'README.md#Why Fugaro',
    // The sentence that says what Fugaro is not: there is no Fugaro service in between, and
    // what it uses is the reader's own project and credentials.
    caption: 'it uses your Google Cloud project and your model credentials',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ephemeral cloud job',
    source: 'README.md#How it works',
    // README.md's ASCII diagram, the far end of the CLI's arrow in its own words.
    caption: 'one execution per task, then gone',
  },
  {
    name: 'Pull request',
    source: 'docs/git-providers.md#Checkpoint pushes (git.pr.checkpoints)',
    // The document that owns when a pull request opens, in its own words. Not README.md's
    // step 4 clause about the push — "each commit is pushed within about a minute while the
    // agent works" — which is the branch's business and read, under this step's name, as the
    // pull request appearing and updating per commit (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19).
    // The line is lowercase and verb-first like the other three, and the section it cites
    // says it in one sentence with the draft state beside it.
    caption: 'the first checkpoint opens the draft PR, marked not verified',
  },
]

// Hold every caption to the section it cites, in that section's order, here, so a
// paraphrase — or a caption that moved to the step above — stops the build naming the file
// rather than reaching a reader as the repository's own words. `cursor` walks forward
// through a section, so two steps citing the same one can only quote it in the order it
// writes them; each step cites a section of its own today, and the rule is what keeps that
// from being the only thing that stops a caption moving.
{
  const cursors: { [name: string]: number } = {}
  for (const [index, step] of STEPS.entries()) {
    const section = SOURCES[step.source]
    const line = comparable(step.caption)
    const from = cursors[step.source] ?? 0
    const at = section ? comparable(section).indexOf(line, from) : -1
    if (at === -1) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro: step ${index + 1}'s caption ${JSON.stringify(step.caption)} is not a line of ${step.source} at or after the step before it, and every caption in the flow is the repository's own words in the order their document writes them (changes spec 2026-10-18, item 4; Global Constraint 6).`,
      )
    }
    cursors[step.source] = at + line.length
  }
}
---

<figure class="flow" aria-label="How a task becomes a pull request" data-source="docs/website/redesign/changes-spec-2026-10-18.md">
  <ol class="flow-steps">
    {
      STEPS.map((step, index) => (
        <li class="flow-step" data-step={step.name.toLowerCase()} data-source={step.source}>
          <div class="step-card">
            <div class="step-head">
              {/* The step's place in the flow, as a number: the list carries the order, so
                  this is decoration and a screen reader reads the `<ol>` instead. */}
              <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">{index + 1}</span>
              <h2 class="step-name">{step.name}</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="step-caption">{step.caption}</p>
          </div>
          {/* The arrow between this step and the next: decoration, and the last step has
              none. It is inside the step rather than between two of them because a list of
              steps may not carry anything but steps. It points down the column and right
              along the row, which is the layout's business rather than the markup's. */}
          {index < STEPS.length - 1 && (
            <svg class="step-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 32" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <path class="arrow-line" d="M12 1 V 25" />
              <path class="arrow-head" d="M4 19 L12 29 L20 19" />
            </svg>
          )}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
</figure>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): the flow's cards, its numbers and its arrows are named through the
     palette rather than given colours of their own. The accent is what marks the picture out
     in its section — the filled step numbers, the rail along the top of each card and the
     arrows are `--accent`, on the ground or on a card (`--accent` against `--ground` and
     against `--surface` are pairs `a11y.mjs` computes at 3:1, and `--ground` on the filled
     number is the 4.5:1 pair for text on the accent) — and what the picture is *made* of is
     the card's size, the surface and the numbers rather than an outline around them (review
     round 5, below). */
  .flow {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--page-max);
  }

  /* `max-width: none` is not decoration: `base.css` holds prose to the measure
     (`:where(p, ul, ol, dl, blockquote, figcaption) { max-width: var(--measure) }`), and the
     flow's steps are an `<ol>`. The cap is 68 ch — about 578 px at the body's 17 px — and the
     four cards with their three arrows want about 720 px, so the list drew the whole picture
     inside a 578 px box: cards about 143 px wide, `Ephemeral cloud job` breaking as
     `Ephemera / l cloud job`, and the flow the narrowest thing in the section it is the
     subject of. The list is not prose; it is the picture, and it is as wide as the page's
     column. `website/test/base-styles.mjs` resolves the cascade for this element's own
     `max-width` and refuses any cap narrower than the row needs, so this line cannot be
     dropped quietly. */
  .flow-steps {
    max-width: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* The step, and the card that fills it. `position: relative` is what the arrow is placed
     against at the three widths it sits in a gap; `align-items: stretch` and the card's
     `flex: 1 1 auto` are what make the four cards one height — the row container stretches
     the steps, and each card stretches to its own step (see the row block below, where the
     defect this replaces is named). */
  .flow-step {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: stretch;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The centrepiece's card (changes spec item 4's acceptance: the picture is the most
     visually prominent element in its section). **The accent is the top rail and nothing
     else.** Every edge used to carry it — 2 px of cyan down both sides and along the bottom,
     6 px along the top — and the review of round 5 found what four cyan boxes in a row do to
     the thing they are meant to frame: the borders competed with the names inside them, and
     the flow read as a grid of boxes rather than as four steps of one run. So the sides and
     the bottom are the site's separator (`--border`, one pixel) and the top is the accent at
     4 px, which is the mark the eye follows across the row, left to right. The prominence
     item 4 asks for is the section's and the card's size, the surface and the number, not the
     loudness of an outline. `--accent` on `--surface` is still a pair `a11y.mjs` computes at
     3:1 for a UI edge, and it is still an edge of every card. */
  .step-card {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-top: calc(var(--border-width) * 4) solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  /* The step's heading row: the number and the name on one line, which is what the phone's
     full-width card has room for. The number is a fixed box beside the name; every layout
     where the cards share the width — the two-by-two and the row — turns this into a column
     instead (see the 35 rem block below), because there the name needs the box. */
  .step-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
  }

  .step-number {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex: none;
    width: 2rem;
    height: 2rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    /* The heaviest weight the token layer names: `--weight-semibold` (600) is the top of the
       scale, so "bold" on this site is this, and a number set in a weight outside the tokens
       would be a second type scale. `--ground` on the filled accent is the 4.5:1 pair
       `a11y.mjs` computes for text on the accent. */
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* The name wraps, at every width it has to: it is a heading, and a heading that cannot fit
     its card runs out of it. What it may not do is wrap in the row, where the four names
     stand beside each other — `Ephemeral cloud job` on two lines while the other three are
     one line each is a card taller than its neighbours, which is the ragged row the review
     of this branch measured. So the row's breakpoint is set where the *name* fits, and
     `website/test/base-styles.mjs` computes that box from the built stylesheet and refuses a
     row that starts before a card can print its longest name on one line. */
  .step-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    /* 20 px (`--font-size-lg`) and `--weight-semibold`: the largest step of the type scale at
       which the longest of item 4's four names — `Ephemeral cloud job`, spelled as the spec
       spells it — is one line inside a quarter of the page's column. The test estimates that
       name's width per character at the size this rule declares and requires a margin on top
       of it, so a larger size here fails the build rather than wrapping on a desktop; and the
       names are still the largest thing in the card below the number, which is what item 4's
       acceptance ("the most visually prominent element in its section") rests on. */
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    color: var(--text);
    /* A name longer than its card — a future step the spec words in one long word — breaks
       rather than running out of the card, the same guard the caption below carries. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The step's one short line, at the body's own size rather than a step below it: the
     caption is what the name is a name for, and at `--font-size-sm` it read as a footnote to
     a heading. `--text-muted` on `--surface` is a pair `a11y.mjs` computes at 4.5:1 in all
     four palettes — the same requirement as body text, not a decorative grey. */
  .step-caption {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    /* A caption is a sentence with a code span in it, and a long unbroken token would push
       the card wider than the page on a phone: it breaks rather than does that. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .step-arrow {
    display: block;
    width: 1.5rem;
    height: 2rem;
    margin: var(--space-2xs) auto;
  }

  .arrow-line,
  .arrow-head {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--accent);
    /* A thick stroke in the accent: the arrows are what says the four boxes are a run rather
       than four facts, so they are drawn at the weight of a rule rather than of a hairline. */
    stroke-width: 3;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* Wherever the cards are narrower than the page's own column — the two-by-two and the row,
     everything from 35 rem up — the number sits above the name rather than beside it: it is
     `flex: none` and 32 px plus the head's gap, which is 44 px of a card's content box that
     the name needs to print itself on one line. Above the name it costs a row and nothing
     horizontal — and a row is a thing a card can afford, a name that does not fit is not. */
  @media (min-width: 35rem) {
    .step-head {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }
  }

  /* The two-by-two: `1 2` over `4 3`, at the widths between a phone's stack and the page's
     own column. Four stacked cards are over 400 px of picture at 768 px, which is what a
     reader scrolls past to reach the run's own lines under it, and four across is not possible yet (see the row
     below): two rows of two are the shape the run has there, and the arrows follow it —
     `1 -> 2` along the top row, `2 -> 3` down its right-hand side, `3 -> 4` back along the
     bottom row. The arrows are absolutely positioned in the gaps for the same reason they
     are in the row: in the flow they would make the two cards of a row different heights,
     because the last step of the flow carries no arrow at all.

     **The bottom row is `4 3` and not `3 4`, which is what makes the down arrow true.** The
     grid used to be `1 2` over `3 4` with step 2's arrow drawn below it: step 2 is above step
     *4* in that grid, so the arrow that leaves step 2 landed on step 4 — the picture read
     `1 -> 2 -> 4`, step 3 had no arrow pointing into it and step 4 had two (review round 4).
     With step 3 under step 2 the arrow lands on its successor, and step 3's own arrow turns
     to point left at step 4, which is now beside it on the other side. The DOM order is
     untouched — it is the run's order, and it is what a screen reader reads; only the
     drawing is a snake. `website/test/base-styles.mjs` reads the grid's own placements, works
     out which cell each step's successor is in, and refuses an arrow drawn on a side of its
     card where the next step does not sit. */
  @media (min-width: 35rem) and (max-width: 71.98rem) {
    .flow-steps {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    }

    /* Steps 3 and 4, placed rather than left to the grid's own row-major order, because the
       run wraps here: step 3 sits under step 2 (the arrow down the right-hand side) and step
       4 to its left, at the end of the run. */
    .flow-step:nth-child(3) {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 2;
    }

    .flow-step:nth-child(4) {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
    }

    .step-card {
      padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-sm);
    }

    .step-arrow {
      position: absolute;
      width: 1rem;
      height: 1rem;
      margin: 0;
    }

    /* A turn of `-90deg` points the drawn-down arrow along the row it travels: a positive
       angle in CSS turns clockwise, so `90deg` would send it back at the step before it. The
       margin is what centres the arrow's box in the gap it is drawn in — half the gap less
       half the box. */
    .flow-step:nth-child(odd) .step-arrow {
      top: 50%;
      left: 100%;
      margin: calc(-1 * var(--space-2xs)) 0 0 calc(var(--space-md) / 2 - var(--space-2xs));
      transform: rotate(-90deg);
    }

    /* The one arrow that goes down the two-by-two: step 2's, from the top row to the step
       below it. `transform` is left alone, which is the direction the drawing is drawn in. */
    .flow-step:nth-child(2) .step-arrow {
      top: 100%;
      left: 50%;
      margin: calc(var(--space-lg) / 2 - var(--space-2xs)) 0 0 calc(-1 * var(--space-2xs));
    }

    /* And the one that turns back along the bottom row: step 3's, at its left edge, pointing
       left at step 4. `90deg` is a quarter turn clockwise, which takes the drawn-down arrow
       to the left — the mirror of the odd rule above, which is the same turn the other way
       for the arrows that travel rightwards. The margin centres the box in the gap on that
       side of the card, and it is negative because the box starts at the card's own edge. */
    .flow-step:nth-child(3) .step-arrow {
      top: 50%;
